My GP I’m missing one of his articles on rare research titled, ‘Money produces brain activations, especially if others get less.’
That is, the feelings generated by the monthly pay of the workers depends on what the companions earn.
The study has been conducted using tomographic MRIs at the University of Bonn, and the results are published in the latest issue of Science.
According to Bernd Weber, author of the study and director of the Neurocognition Imaging group at the Life&Brain Institute in Germany: "The strongest activation occurred when the participant responded correctly and his partner failed."
The researchers observed that if when both were successful they received the same pay, the activation was moderate, but if one received twice as much, the activation was higher.
I don’t know if I required this conclusion so much science.
The Prosecutor of the Balkans
Carla del Ponte has been relentlessly chasing the Balkan war criminals for eight years since the Prosecutor’s Office of the Criminal Tribunal for the Former Yugoslavia, located in The Hague.
His term ends at the end of the year and Carla will leave with a bittersweet taste, because those mainly responsible for the Srebrenica massacre - in which 8,000 Bosnian Muslims died-, former General Ratko Mladic and former Bosnian Serb leader Radovan Karadzic, remain free.
Their names are on that list from which the documentary of the Swiss filmmaker Marcel Schüpbach, 'La Lista de Carla' (2006, not released in Spain) that, with the rhythm of a police thriller, follows the work of the Swiss prosecutor from Belgrade to New York to persecute the escaped criminals and pressure the governments involved to tighten the encirclement.
What's surely that suits me?
Car insurance, which covers the driver before possible accidents, supposes an important expense that will depend on various factors, such as age, sex, age of the card, marital status, the type of vehicle to be insured or the use of it, among others.
Having the car is just the beginning.
Choosing the right car insurance is even more difficult than choosing the vehicle model.
First of all you have to be clear about what we are talking about.
Car insurance is an agreement between an insurance company and the insured.
This commitment materializes in an insurance contract, which establishes that the company receives the value of the agreed premium in exchange for being responsible for the obligation to pay compensation in the event of termination.
The premium is the price of insurance, which is established taking into account various factors, such as age, sex, card age, marital status, the type of vehicle to be insured or the use given to it, among others.
The insurance companies set their prices according to the statistics, so that the greater the probability of suffering an accident, the greater the quota to be paid.
That’s why those older than 25 and women have to pay lower premiums, as statistics reflect that they have a lower accident rate due to their prudence.
In addition, taking advantage of recounts, companies have concluded that married people tend to have fewer accidents than single drivers.
The use given to the vehicle will influence the frequency, and with it on the probability of having some kind of mishap.
By increasing the frequency the odds increase.
There are many variables that insurers take into account when it comes to setting the premium price.
That’s why the driver has to study the situation well to choose that car insurance that best fits their profile.
We will present here the most significant variables when choosing one or another type of insurance, so that this, in addition to adapting to the characteristics of the insured, offers you a reasonable price.
Young people under the age of 25 find themselves with various inconveniences when it comes to hiring insurance at an affordable price, such as reduced driving experience and card age.
Although the ideal to be well insured is full insurance, there are other cheaper options.
The most cautious drivers can opt for an all-risk with franchise, so that they get all-risk insurance coverage but at a more feasible price.
This type of agreement would mean that the client pays a part of the amount of the accident.
"The part of the amount of the accident that the customer contributes will be pre-established according to the value of the premium, which will be higher the lower the amount," specifies Luis Noguera, head in Spain of First Europe, online insurance mediator.
Another option is extended third-party insurance, so that the customer ensures protection against damage caused to third parties, although not before those caused in their own car.
In addition, it would be covered by theft, fire and vehicle loss.
A piece of advice.
If you are under 25 try to be careful and avoid claims as far as possible, since this will have an impact on the bonus, being able to reduce the cost of insurance in the future.
A bonus is a reduction on the base rate the company owns.
On the contrary, a penalty is a surcharge on that base rate.
"The bonuses and penalties will depend on the company and the company's profit.
If the company has had a good year it will be able to allow itself to increase the bonuses, "clarifies Noguera.
After the 25-year-old range, drivers have it easier when it comes to taking out insurance, since the accident rate is reduced thanks to the accumulated experience.
For this reason, it is most advisable to hire an all-risk with franchise.
"Normally young people can not make use of the experience previously acquired when it comes to being used in an accident, and even when it comes to driving," says the director in Spain of the European company, to which he adds that the majority of accidents occur in the second year of driving, once after the 'fear of the rookie'.
However, if the client is married but does not yet have children it is preferable to negotiate insurance at full risk, as long as their economic situation allows it, of course.
For their part, marriages with children are considered by insurers as the public with the lowest accident rate, so the surcharges will be lower.
In this case the best insurance would be one at all risk with franchise (coulding to be this of 300 euros), or, when a high level of bonus is available, one to an extended third party from the third year of life of the vehicle.
"They carry out a more relaxed driving and have greater purchasing power to allow themselves the luxury of assuming a greater amount of franchise," says Luis Noguera.
For retirees it is advisable a full-risk insurance with a franchise (this being 150 euros), or one to extended third parties when you have a high level of bonus.
It must be said that car insurance is something important, not only because it covers the driver before possible accidents, but also because it represents an important expense, which is why it is essential to adequately study the insurance that best suits us according to our personal situation.
Celtic are looking in Milan for a point worth the eighth
Milan and Celtic are measured this Tuesday in San Siro in search of the point they lack to achieve their respective objectives in the closing of the 'Champions' league, since the Italians are used to be first and the Scots to sentence their classification.
The Italians add up to ten points and already certified in the fifth day their pass with a tie in Portugal, while Celtic have nine points, but they still need another to make sure that Shakhtar, with six, and who receives Benfica, who has four, does not advance him.
These two Group D matches will be played a week before the end of the sixth day, as Milan have to play the Club World Cup in Japan, where they debut the 13th in the semi-finals in search of a place for the final of 16.
Thus, this group will be closed before the rest and the draw in the Giuseppe Meazza seems the most anticipated result, although the two teams will try to go out for a victory, since the Italians will not want to speculate at home and Celtic knows that winning would come first and would ensure a better crossing in the eighth.
Gordon Strachan's team could even serve a loss to qualify if Shakhtar does not win, but will not be able to be trusted because on an equal nine points the pass is for the Ukrainians, who lost last week in Glasgow (2-1), but won (2-0) in the other direct duel.
Thus, the Milanists will try to bitter the night to the Scots, who hope to get into eighths for the second time in the restructured Champions League and consecutively, after they were precisely defeated in that round last season with a goal from Kaka in the extension (1-0) after the 0-0 of the first leg.
Waiting the victory of the 'rossoneri' will be Shakhtar who is only worth the victory, in the event that he loses Celtic, both to get into eighths and to continue in UEFA.
Benfica, despite being last and without options to continue in the maximum European competition, could reach third place with a victory, with which UEFA would play.
20,000 people attend the first Spice Girls tour concert in Vancouver
The Spice Girls have started in Vancouver (Canada), in front of about 20,000 spectators, their first tour since they dissolved in 2000.
'Posh Spice' (Victoria Beckham), 'Scary Spice' (Melanie Brown), 'Baby Spice' (Emma Bunton), 'Sporty Spice' (Melanie Chisholm) and 'Ginger Spice' (Geri Halliwell) declared shortly before they started their concert at GM Place in Vancouver that the group's meeting is "brilliant".
About 20,000 people sold out the tickets to witness the concert that included a video that tells the story of the group that made furor at the end of the 1990s.
The reunion and the world tour coincide with the release of a new album that collects their main hits as well as two new songs, 'Headlines' and 'Voodoo', which in Spain publishes EMI.
The five members of Spice Girls took to the stage wearing bracelets that had been given by the husband of 'Posh Spice', the British footballer David Beckham.
The athlete was among the public that crowded the GM Place, even though Victoria’s nerves had been banned days earlier.
In the lead-up to the start of the show, Victoria Beckham pointed out that the British star "hasn't seen our costumes at all, so I'm nervous about what he's going to think of the show."
'Sporty Spice' whose departure of the group in 1998 "because of the differences between us" caused the dissolution of the quintet two years later, he also expressed his nervousness during an encounter with the press, two hours before the start of the show.
"She was scared.
I was nervous but I saw that the feeling of the world has changed, the feeling for the Spice Girls has changed.
And I thought not, so I’m happy to have made the decision,” Chisholm said of the group meeting she dissolved nine years ago.
The singer also pointed out that over time, "things have been fixed and we love each other very much."
But a lot has changed since the Spice Girls last performed together in 1998.
According to CTV television, the group arrived in Vancouver on a special Virgin Airlines plane.
Upon his arrival at the airport, an army of nannies awaited them.
Among the five, there are seven sons.
After the Vancouver concert, the group will perform in California (USA), in San Jose (December 4) and Los Angeles (December 5 and 7).
In Madrid they will perform on December 23.
New Fears of Gas Between Russia and Ukraine
The growing dispute over the price of gas between Ukraine and Russia increases the risk that it will affect Russia’s export of fuel to Western Europe.
Most of the gas exported by Russia to the European Union (EU) is directed through Ukraine, so each friction between both nations is a source of attention.
Kiev has warned that if Moscow raises the price of gas, Russia will have to pay higher transit rates.
A previous dispute last year reduced supplies to EU states.
The last dispute between Kiev and Moscow came with the Ukrainian government and the Russian state, monopoly gas owner Gazprom, trying to agree on the amount of money Ukraine had to pay for Russian gas in 2008.
Gazprom declared that it will charge Ukraine more, since it has pledged to pay an extra 50% in 2008 for the gas that comes from neighboring Turkmenistan.
Analysts say it could be that Ukraine pays 40% more for Gazprom imports.
“It’s important for us to use our arguments as one of the most important transits for gas during the talks,” Ukrainian Economy Minister Anatoly Kinakh told Reuters.
"Changes in gas prices should not relate to our arguments on levels of transit rates [to Western Europe] via Ukraine."
The last great dispute over gas between Moscow and Kiev, Gazprom cut the supply to Ukraine in January 2006.
This meant a drop in exports to Western Europe, which quickly raised prices.
Russian gas accounts for a quarter of the EU’s supply.
Russia has the world’s largest gas reserves, but much of it is not yet fully developed.
As a result, Gazprom complements its own gas supply from Turkmenistan and other former Central Asian Soviet nations.
NHS "should test foreign workers"
The Scottish NHS needs to monitor criminal records for employees outside of Europe, a vigilante said.
Auditor-General Robert Black’s recommendation came after a foreign doctor was found to be related to the attack on Glasgow airport in June.
Black also expressed concern that the Scottish NHS has no exact idea of how many foreign personnel it has.
However, their report noted other checkpoints that are carried out correctly through a sample of five health authorities.
The auditor-general checked the amount of foreign personnel hired outside the European economic area in the wake of attacks on Glasgow airport.
Bilal Abdullah, 28, an Iraqi doctor at Paisley’s Royal Alexandra Hospital, was arrested at the airport after crashing a jeep at the gates of the airport’s terminal building.
He was one of three doctors acting at the Old Bailey by video conference in October, indicted in connection with alleged car bombings in Britain.
NHS expects to carry out pre-employment check-ups on candidates, to ensure that they meet qualifications, experience and authorization to work in the UK.
Although Scotland’s health board uses “Disclosure Scotland” to carry out criminal background checks, the agency has no jurisdiction to obtain this information outside Britain.
The auditor-general’s report recommends, among other measures, that boards should have guidance from the Criminal Records Bureau, which assists employers with criminal checks in many countries outside Britain.
"Although we found some of the personal records with evidence of these controls, this is not a routine review," the report says.
Black states that the Scottish NHS has a high level of compliance with its own employment policies, but notes that: “Our report highlights areas that can be improved to ensure that the necessary information is available in the number of NHS foreign personnel.”
BMA Scotes President Dr Peter Terry said it was "very unlikely" that pre-employment checks could identify potential NHS employees who could commit terrorist acts in the future.
"The report suggests that pre-employment check-ups work at NHS Scotland but it's not clear what the added value of the recommendations made is," he said.
The Scottish health authorities state that they currently have 1,161 foreigners, most of them doctors and nurses
But the auditor-general warned that the number may be higher, in part because the boards have trouble identifying which employees have indefinite residence authorizations.
The investigation also found that the verification procedures established by NHS authorities in Ayrshire and Arran, Grampian, Greater Glasgow and Clyde, Lanarkshire and Lothian were being widely followed.
This includes passport checks, work permitism, and qualification checking.
The Scottish government is currently reviewing the pre-employment policy and guidance of aliens in the NHS.
Health Secretary Nicola Sturgeon said: "He had a keen interest in carrying out this work in the wake of the terrorist attack at Glasgow Airport.
He added that the Scottish government, in conjunction with the health boards, could consider the recommendations of the reports and that this will be reflected in the update of pre-employment control guidelines for all working groups - including foreigners - what they planned to publish next month.
Joy Division dominates in film awards
Control, the biographical film about Joy Division’s late vocalist Ian Curtis has won five awards at the British Independent Film Festival, including the Best Picture Award.
The trophies were also given to protagonist Sam Riley, who worked in a warehouse until he was chosen for the role of Curtis, for director Anton Corbijn and co-star Toby Kebbell.
Actress Judi Dench was named Best Actress for the thriller "Notes of a Scandage."
And Bond's protagonist Daniel Craig won the variety award for putting a British film on the international stage.
The awards were presented at a ceremony at the London Roundhouse.
Control follows Curtis' rise to fame with Joy Division until his suicide in 1980 at the age of 23.
The black-and-white film that features a soundtrack by The Killers, David Bowie and New Order – was produced with only 3 thousand pounds sterling.
But also the Dutch photographer Corbijn, already known by the iconic cover of U2’s Joshua Tree album, awarded with two awards – for best director and best debuting director, was also winning.
Rock music was at the center of another award-winning at the ceremony—such as Julien Temple’s film about The Clash vocalist Joe Strummer won the best documentary award.
Viggo Mortensen for best actor was won for his role as a driver of a Russian mafia boss from London in David Cronenberg's film Eastern Promises.
Ray Winstone, who has a reputation for playing hard, received the Richard Jarris Award for his contribution to British cinema.
Fiftieth, whose films include Scum, Nil By Mouth and Sexy Beast, currently film the Indiana Jones sequel "The Kingdom of the Crystal Skull".
Winstone said he was proud to receive that award, named in honor of Camelot and Harry Potter star Harris, who died in 2002.
"It was people like Richard Harris and Albert Finney who opened doors to people like me," he said.
"I was fortunate to meet Richard Harris a couple of times.
He was a great guy, a great actor of our time, it's really an honor."
Independent film awards, known as BIFAS, are selected by a team that includes filmmakers, actors and festival organizers.
Supporters and patrons of the Bifa festival include Mike Figgis, Ken Loach, Ewan McGregor, Samantha Morton, Bill Nighy, Meera Syal and Michael Winterbottom.
Saudi Arabia arrests 208 terrorists
Saudi Arabia revealed yesterday that it arrested 208 militants of terrorist cells planning an impending attack on oil facilities, as well as attacks on religious and security forces.
State television of the world’s largest oil provider said one of the cells was planning missile smuggling.
Al-Qaeda sympathizers have staged a campaign against the local monarchy, in alliance with the United States, since 2003 after the invasion of Iraq.
A cell of eight militants made up of foreign residents was planning an attack on an oil facility in the eastern province.
Saudi Arabia created a force of 35,000 troops to protect the facilities of Abqaiq, the world’s largest oil plant, after a failed al-Qaeda attack in 2006.
The report, citing a statement by the Saudi Interior Minister, said 18 of those arrested belong to a cell led by a “missile launch expert,” who has entered the country.
The minister said the militants had planned smuggling entry of eight shells into the country.
Another 22 of the detainees were planning murders of religious leaders and security forces.
The government has warned religious leaders that they must be more active to stop the Saudis who travel to Iraq to unite as militants to Al-Qaeda to fight the Allied forces and to back the Muslim Shiite government, which is considered heretic by the most extreme Saudi Sunnis.
Al-Qaeda militants target many religious in Saudi Arabia who have been appointed by authorities to support the policies of the royal family, which dominates the government.
The militants - recruited by calls from Osama bin Laden, the Saudi-born al Qaeda leader, to harass the pro-Western government of the country - aimed for residences of foreigners associated with the government, government buildings and energy facilities.
"This was a great effort of the security forces, for five months of work," the interior minister's spokesman told state television.
Those arrested also include a "mediatic cell" of 16 people in Medina, whose objective was to promote "takfiri thinking" - the ideology of radical Sunni Muslims that supports violence against infidels and apostates.
The arrest includes 32 others accused of providing financial support and support to the militants.
Climate of Venus can give an idea of Earth's future
Venus may have once been an appropriate place for life, scientists said yesterday.
Data from an in-orbiting European spacecraft showed the planet more close to an Earth twin than previously observed.
Venus is the closest planet to Earth.
The two planets are similar in size and occupy similar orbits, although Venus is slightly closer to the sun.
Millions of years ago, Venus may have had conditions similar to those that made Earth a habitable planet, scientists now believe.
But the effects of carbon dioxide and the greenhouse effect, atmospheric erosion by solar radiation, and the slower solar rotation sent Venus to a very different kind of development.
Today the planet has no water, the surface temperature is enough to melt lead and is eroded by hurricane winds and lightning storms.
The results of the European Space Agency for the Venus Express, which has studied the planet for a year, offers an overview of how the Earth could develop catastrophic changes due to the climate.
Professor Fred Taylor, from the Department of Physics at the University of Oxford and one of the leaders of the Venus Express mission, states: "It is clarifying why the climate on Venus is so different from that of the Earth, when they are two such similar planets.
"Our new data makes it possible to build a scenario in which Venus began as Earth, possibly with a habitable environment millions of years ago."
Police confiscation "enriched uranium"
A kilo of radioactive material, which they declare is enriched uranium, was confiscated yesterday by Slovak and Hungarian police.
A police spokesman stated that three people were arrested and the material was examined.
The cash could not confirm whether it was enriched uranium but said it could have been sold for 1 million dollars (480,000 sterling pounds).
It's still open
Mitt Romney says it’s not worthy for presidential candidates to answer questions from a snow monkey.
However, Republican candidates for the presidency eventually agree to hold a debate on YouTube, in which ordinary voters record questions on video and send them for dissemination among the candidates.
None of the questions put on Wednesday afternoon Nov. 28 were more ridiculous than that of the snow monkey who asked Democrats about climate warming in YouTube’s Republican party debate.
But the questions, and the answers, this time reflected much of the state of the Republican party ahead of next year’s elections.
It’s not known exactly how CNN producers, who co-sponsor the debate, chose the YouTube videos to show the candidates for candidates.
But the first half-hour debate, the longest devoted to a single issue, focused on immigration.
The issue is one of the most powerful topics for Republicans.
CNN called on a group of indecisive voters to raise the dial when they heard ideas they liked: broadcasters noticed spikes in approval when candidates spoke with extremist arguments.
The toughest word exchanges of the night came between Rudy Giuliani, the national pioneer, and Mr. Romney, who has been leading in the crucial polls of Iowa and New Hampshire.
Mr. Giuliani is trying to ruin Romney’s reputation as he was soft with illegal immigration when he was mayor of New York.
He attacked Mr. Romney, accusing the former governor of hiring illegal immigrants to work at his home.
Both responded aggressively when asked if any candidate could support an “amnesty” (allowing illegals to regularize their situation and giving them the possibility to obtain citizenship).
Minor candidates such as Duncan Hunter, a California congressman, and Tom Tancredo, a Colorado congressman, reached agreement on the issue.
None has the word of being nominated, but both remain in the race perhaps because they both warned about the issue of immigration earlier than the others.
At least in immigration, and in general terms, candidates share views: increased border surveillance, intolerance with illegal immigrants.
This topic, coupled with fears of China’s toxic toys and other foreign concerns, helps Republicans forget, or minimize, divisions between liberals and Christian conservatives.
But the debate became awkward when other issues appeared.
Mr. Giuliani was asked why he had supported gun control in the past.
John McCain and Mr. Romney debated whether immersion in water should be considered torture.
One person asked if the candidates believed in every word in the Bible.
This led Mr. Giuliani to stumble into the position that while the Bible was his favorite book, he didn’t literally believe in the history of the Jonah and the whale.
Another asked if Jesus would support the death penalty.
Mike Huckabee – now, against all expectations, first in Iowa’s most recent poll, thanks to his long-standing Christian conservatism – said the death penalty was painful but necessary.
When he was pressured to answer if Jesus would be in favor, he laughed saying that Jesus was too smart to be a candidate in the elections.
The race remains unusually open.
Mr. Huckabee is ramping up in Iowa, coinciding with liberal Ron Paul’s fundraising campaigns, and his heated statements from Wednesday’s debate.
The two have come out from the bottom, because the two wings of the party did not agree on the most famous and traditional candidate.
In 1980 and 2000, a candidate united the party early and convincingly with a winning message about America's greatness, satisfying both party wings.
This time the party is divided and the outcome is far from clear.
Labourist Sorrows
Gordon Brown has discovered another hole in which to sink; but it also emerges
As political scandals continue in England, it is not particularly interesting.
They don’t seem to have sold influences, nor are they exorbitant hotel beads in Paris, or sucks of extra-marital fingers.
But it has been revealed that a wealthy businessman has disguised contributions to the English Labour governing party of more than 650,000 sterlings (US$1.3 million), for about four years using fake names, and that the party didn’t denounce.
Two months ago, when the prime minister eluded calling for an election, stupidly insulting British intelligence declaring that opinion polls didn’t influence his decision, the hope was that he would provide a strong and efficient government.
But instead, like Vince Calbe, the liberal Democrat leads with growing ingenuity, because the dreaded Mr. Brown has gone from Stalin to being Mr. Bean.
The bailout of Northern Rock, a troubled bank, demonstrated regulatory weakness and judgmental poverty in times of crisis.
Bad proposals to change the tax code have put business groups together against the government.
The tax authority lost 25 million personal data revealed an unimaginable administrative slop.
A trio of retired generals began a mutiny in the House of Lords, protesting against cuts in military spending: being ministers of the armed forces is, they claimed, a part-time job.
Now it’s the multiple donations from David Abrahams; Peter Watt, Labour’s (and former accomplical) secretary-general has already resigned.
None of this means that Mr. Brown irrevocably walks towards the exit.
Governments can reach a tipping point after they find themselves unable to govern.
People don’t believe them as politicians, but they usually suspend their disbelief when a new lot takes control.
Once it is clear that the prime minister cannot improve things, which does not subtract much time, that suspension is too much: public officials begin to fail; cabinet ministers are exposed; the press looks for bad news; and the government becomes defensive and does not focus.
On the one hand, Brown’s position is particularly difficult, because unlike other political leaders he can’t defend himself by arguing that it’s new.
Even if I wanted to deny Tony Blair, it was his superior lieutenant.
The problems of Northern Rock and authority registration tax are related to Brown’s decisions as chancellor of the treasury.
And even though he’s not directly involved in Blairish’s setbacks – or “partneer cash,” political party funding research — voters can assume that if it is.
However, Mr. Brown's situation isn't as bad as it seems.
Plates aren't as bad as the Tories want to show.
Like in Mr. Bean, no one has ever died, nor has he been scammed directly.
And the remedy, albeit painful, is to give what Mr. Brown’s naturally Stalinist tendencies have always promised: a reliable and brutal government.
The new political scandal of donations is a good example.
Mr. Abrahams, who likens his donations to charitable gifts, says he doesn’t want public recognition for his benevolence.
This may be the case, but he doesn't make the decision.
Anti-anonymity laws for big political taxpayers is clear and exists for a clear reason: the public has the right to know who is buying access to those in power.
Mr. Watt, who said he knew of Mr. Abraham's agreement, and which was not illegal, will obviously confront Jon Mendlesohn, head of fundraiser who was asked about donations in October.
But Mr. Brown so far isn’t implicated: there’s nothing to suggest that he knew about these performances, although the bulk of the payments were made after he took control of the office in June.
So far the prime minister has done half the job.
He has been quick to convict his party for violating the law, but is diluted in decision-making for a family matter, a commission of inquiry into the matter—led largely by a judge and a bishop.
In this case, morals are not complicated.
If the law has been breached, it's a police matter.
In addition, there are other things that Mr. Brown must do, such as finding a permanent and independent leader for the Public Life Standards Commission, since he has not had one for six months, and he must also push for a genuine reform of party financing.
This will require an arduous cooperative work of Labour with the Tories, and perhaps a renunciation of the special financial situation with the unions—bitter measures for a partisan like Mr. Brown.
But he must deal with some partisan facts.
Conservatives now have a good level of support in the polls; he has two years to win voters; and the only way he can move to victory is to act with impeccable probity and strong competition.
Sharks cards
Is the subprime mortgage crisis infecting the U.S. credit card market?
When there is blood in the water, it is natural that dorsal finds are walking around.
Today that the American housing market is in crisis, predators have their sights set on the country’s market for credit cards.
Analysts at Goldman Sachs estimate that credit card losses can rise to US$99 million if the subprime mortgage crisis infects other forms of consumer credit.
Signs of contagion are already seen.
There is an increase in both card charges and crime rates, which measures the proportion of balances that have not been amortized or that are more than 30 days late respectively.
HSBC announced last month that it has taken 1.4 million charge in its U.S. consumer financing business, in part due to card borrower weakness.
It's too early for panic, though.
Delinquency and crime are at low levels.
According to Moody's, a qualifying agency, the third quarter the 3.89% crime rate was almost one percentage point below the historical average.
Deterioration in rates can be partly explained by technical factors.
A change in U.S. people’s bankruptcy law (2005) led to a sharp drop in bankruptcy applications, which in turn represent a major loss in credit cards; the number of applications (and therefore delinquency) would be growing again, or it may happen that conditions for borrowers were worsening.
The industry also reports strong payment rates, which show how much of their debt consumers pay each month.
And confidence in credit cards and asset-backed securities is pretty firm, despite paralysis in other financial areas.
TowerGroup’s Dennis Moroney, a research firm, predicts that emission volumes for 2007 will be about 25% higher than last year.
The direct channels of crisis infection between subprime mortgages and the credit card market certainly exist: consumers like to burden credit card debt now that lenders are expiring.
But credit card issuers look more at the flow of liquidity than active securities, so that falling prices don’t necessarily trigger a change in borrowers’ crediting capacity.
They can even work to outperform issuers.
Incentives for consumers to continue amortizing the mortgage decreases if properties are worth less than the value of the loan; and as a result, the payment of the card’s debt rises on the priority list.
Card issuers can also respond quickly and flexibly to the difficult conditions that mortgage lenders, changing interest rates, or altering credit balances.
That in theory should reduce the risk of a rapid asset retaival.
“We’re not going to wake up one day and re-evaluate all the loans,” says Gary Perlin, chief financial officer at Capital One.
If a sudden subprime infection in the credit card market is unlikely, the risks of a sustained decline are much more real.
If home prices are lower and a credit contraction push America into recession, the industry is undoubtedly facing a harsher future.
You have to keep an eye on those dorsal fins.
British bankers found guilty in Enron case
Three British bankers who went on trial for their liability in frauds with Enron’s former head of financial office Andrew Fastow were found guilty on Wednesday.
David Bermingham, Giles Darby and Gary Mulgrew were originally found innocent on seven counts of fraud for colluding with Fastow in a secret financial operation in 2000 to enrich themselves at the expense of employees.
They were sent on trial in January.
But during the hearing before U.S. District Judge Ewing Werlein Jr., each was convicted as guilty of fraud collusion.
The three former executives of Greenwich Natwest, a unit of the Scottish group PLC at the Royal Bank, became a celebrated cause in Britain for extradition proceedings that lasted two years.
In the U.S., his case marks the loose end of an investigation launched after Enron’s collapse in 2001.
Initially, charges against the bankers were to have warned Fastow in 2002 that he was the target of a government investigation into Enron’s fall.
The indictment against the bankers - dubbed "NatWest Three" - alleges that they came to Houston in 2000 to invent a fraudulent scheme with Fastow and his previous leader Michael Kopper.
Greenwich Natwest had invested in a subsidiary of a Fastow-controlled Enron society, which was the architect of a myriad of frauds in Enron, which aided its spiral from falling into bankruptcy.
In 2002, the bank valued the interests of the subsidiary at zero, but the three Britons knew it would value upwards.
A company under Kopper’s control bought the subsidiary’s bank interest in $1 million.
The bankers paid Kopper 250,000 dollars for interest in that company.
According to the indictment, Fastow falsely represented Enron, since the energy company would pay $20 million to Greenwich NatWest for its parts of the subsidiary.
But the 20 million remained in the hands of British bankers, Fastow and others.
Bankers charged 7.3 million, while Fastow, Kopper and others received about 12.3 million, according to the indictment.
In January 2004, Fastow was found guilty on two counts of conspiracy in Enron's collapse.
The British trio was arrested three months later.
Fastow is serving a 6-year sentence in a Louisiana federal prison.
Kopper is serving a sentence of 3 years and a month in Texarkana.
Each of the three British bankers must pay 1 million dollars they required to live in the United States while the trial was taking place.
Enron, one of the nation’s seven largest companies, declared bankrupt in December 2001 after years of accounting tricks, couldn’t hide the debt of millions or make their failing businesses appear successful.
The collapse lost thousands of jobs, more than 60 million in market values and more than two million in pension plans.
Enron founder Kenneth Lay and former chief executive Jeffrey Skilling were commissioned last year’s prisoners for their roles in the company’s collapse.
Skilling is serving a sentence of more than 24 years.
Lay’s conviction for conspiracy, fraud and other charges was suspended when she died of a heart attack last year.
Neither down nor out in Moscow
The price of a Gulfstream G550 aircraft, equipped with a Rolls-Royce engine and enough fuel capacity to fly directly from Moscow to Los Angeles, starts at $54 million.
But this is a small expense for some of the wealthy Russians.
“It has up to 18 passengers,” says Dan Firer, Gulfstream representative.
"But none asks for that model of so many seats because it's for private family flights or for presidents of a company."
Mr. Firer was in charge of the Gulfstream booth last Friday at the Millionaire Fair, a four-day consumer orgy held at an event center nearing the capital.
In addition to Gulfstream, 200 other luxury suppliers had installed their display stands, hoping to lure new wealthy Russians into all sorts of products, from mahogany-craved yachts to diamond-encrusted car grills, worthy of 50 cents.
Mr. Firer cautiously looks at a shiny orange Porsche at the stand next to him."
"It's a beautiful car, but it's nickel and calderilla compared to us," he said.
“After the United States, Russia is the second-largestest market for private jets.”
Provided with fuel by the oil boom, the Russian capital resembles Dallas when it was in a heyday.
“We lived the same thing in 1890, when some wealthy Americans came to meet in Europe, where they were seen as materialists,” says Marshall Goldman, a senior lecturer at the Davis Center for Russian and Eurasian studies at Harvard University.
"Like the Saudis of the 70's and the Japanese in the 90's, the Russians are emerging as the world's largest consumers and spenders."
The world’s most exclusive luxury brands are looking for their share of the cake.
Elsewhere in Moscow, a conference on the luxury industry will be held this week with Donatella Versace, Tom Ford and Bernard Arnault, president of LVMH, to exchange ideas on how to sell luxury goods in markets like Russia.
(The conference is organized by the International Herald Tribune, owned by the New York Times Company)
Russia has 53 billionaires who make a total of $282 billion, according to the Forbes list of the wealthiest people.
In addition, the wealthy second-graders include 103,000 Russian millionaires whose money reaches $670 billion, according to a study of the evolution of personal wealth in the former Soviet Union, written by economists from the Center for Economic and Financial Research, a local think.
(The World Bank says 15.8 percent of Russians in 2005 were below subsistence.)
“There are hundreds of millionaires in Moscow – and that’s why we’re all here,” says Nicole Marais, sales and marketing representative for David Morris, the London jeweler.
She attends the fair for the first time with the brand’s Russian distributor, the Soho Jewelry group.
"Women are impeccably prepared and men are very worthy."
Visiting the fair on Sunday night, dressed in afternoon costumes, two real estate agents, Evgenia Yumatova, 21, and Svetlana Turovtsova, 26, described their clients and themselves as millionaires.
"I looked at the cars and I'm trying to decide between the Infiniti, the Mercedes and the Porsche," said Yumatova, who reeled off the design suit he wore as a red carpet veteran dishing with Joan Rivers.
The fair aims to attract the rich, the kind of buyers who would be willing to stay several hours in a warehouse that mix marks from Cadillac Escalades, platinum pens, Mövenpick ice cream, custom pool tables, apartments in Dubai and teddy bears from Gund.
The general public can buy 30 euro tickets ($44.57).
"The Russians are interested in learning which brand is the most expensive and the best," said Mr. Gijrath, who sat patriotically at a stand in the Dutch royal porcelains Delft, in front of a mural-sized reconstruction of a Rembrandt painting, made of hand-painted tiles.
“Of course, the Russians are more interested in ‘bling-bling.’
In fact, sellers said that the most-trained rich Russians (10 to 15 years, by Russian standards) have changed their tastes from eye-catching logos to limited-edited brands, which would not be exposed at a trade show.
However, several thousand people: cash millionaires, emissaries of millionaires or tourists, fill the corridors every day.
And some of them buy.
On the first night of last Thursday, Roman Ershov, managing director of King Koil beds, said he sold two $70,000 mattress systems each, made of silk, cashmere and cotton interwoven with gold threads.
Also on the first night, Mikhail Lyubiumov, sales manager for A1-Moto, which equips high-end cars with ad hoc alarm systems, said the company had sold the brilliant orange Porsche for $170,000 to a Russian millionaire.
Sunday afternoon, Andrei Karapetian, president of L.A. Connection, a company that modifies luxury cars, said he had sold three chain bracelet-shaped car grilles for Rolls Royce Phantom at $55,000 each.
He showed the reporter a car wheel, designed for the owner of a Russian casino, with beams embedded with Swarovski-colored crystals, so that it resembled a roulette wheel.
Cost: $22,000 for four wheels.
"The Russian millionaire who wants ostrich leather wheels or a portrait of his wife made of zirconium cubes, is not necessarily going to drive on those wheels," says Mr. Karapetian.
“He’s going to keep the car in his garage, along with another 30 or 40 cars.”
Even if many of his Russian clients are wealthy, Mr. Karapetian clarifies that the phrase "Russian millionaire" does not mean exclusivity, given the wave of real estate prices.
"If you bought an apartment in Moscow three or four years ago, you're probably a millionaire, at least on paper if you're not in cash," he said.
"There's more of them than you think."
Non-millionaires could at least pretend to be rich over the support of the Alliance Continental, a Russian asset management firm.
The company made four red (British-style) phone booths stuffed with billion-dollar fake banknotes.
Azad Karryev, the company’s marketing manager, said his team had printed 500,000 counterfeit banknotes for the Millionaire Fair.
Visitors stopped to shake stacks of money, asking their friends to take photos with their phones while resting on piles of money, or pocketing a couple of millions.
“You see, in Moscow, everyone can feel like a millionaire,” Karryev says.
"Sloppy" surgery could be the cause of death in New Jersey
It was the day before Thanksgiving when Ronald Livingston looked through the window of the "A Little Healthier" restaurant and saw an ambulance rise up White Cape Cod, two doors down West Scott Avenue.
Suddenly, a car appeared from the policeman's patrol.
And then another one.
"Then I saw the ambulance rise," Mr. Livingston, the restaurant's supervisor, said Wednesday.
"That's when I knew someone had died."
Authorities learned that the dead woman was Olga Arroyo, 41, an aide to the Newark police department director.
Investigators are now trying to determine if she died due to a procedure performed by an unauthorized plastic surgeon, who is not yet identified by police, but who works at the home of 221 West Scott.
Authorities still don’t determine what caused Ms. Arroyo’s death, but they wait for the toxicological test although the results won’t be available until a few weeks. This will help solve the mysterious death that haunts the top ranks of the Newark police officer.
Ms. Arroyo had been an aide to police director Garry F. McCarthy since September 2006, and her voice was familiar to many in the apartment.
“She was loyal, hard-working and tenacious,” Mr. McCarthy says of Arroyo in a phone interview Wednesday.
“She was my guardian.”
She was protecting me."
Mr. McCarthy said Arroyo was the wife of Jimmy Arroyo, a former Newark police officer who works for the Alcoholic Beverage Control Commission, who left two sons aged 14 and 11, and a stepdaughter.
Eileen Walsh, a spokeswoman for County Union Attorney Theodore J. Romankow, said detectives are trying to determine if Ms. Arroyo died following an unauthorized surgical procedure.
"That's one of the possibilities," Walsh said.
“It’s an ongoing investigation.”
Authorities said the house arrived near 3 P.M.
On November 21, they found Mrs. Arroyo on a couch.
She was declared dead at the scene.
It’s been more than a week and authorities have said little about their investigation or about the owner of the house, called Flor Margarita Castro.
Ms. Walsh couldn't tell if Ms. Castro has been interrogated by authorities.
Investigators are trying to determine which part of the home was used as a doctor’s office.
On Wednesday, no one responded to the door at Mrs. Castro’s home, where an withered rose bush is located in the front of the house, and a Dalmatian barks in the back.
A blue drum labeled "ethyl vanillin" - used as a substitute for vanilla in food and perfumes - was at the garage door, next to a door leading to the basement.
Two mountain bikes hang from hooks to one side.
Mr McCarthy said that when he arrived in Newark after 25 years in the New York police department, Mrs. Arroyo was invaluable in getting to know the new island city and its rough political culture.
"She would say, 'You have to keep an eye on this or that one,'" he said.
"She was always here when I was, and also when I wasn't.
It's embarrassing."
The federal reserve is expected to cut rates to raise the stock exchange
Shares jumped on both sides of the Atlantic yesterday after the market waited for the federal reserve to soothe credit conditions with cuts to interest rates, which increased with calming comments from the U.S. central bank vice president.
The exchanges loosened in London, New York and through Europe after Donald Kohn, number two of Ben Bernanke, president of the reserve, declared that policymakers must be "flexible and pragmatic" in responding to the continued tension of financial markets.
In a clear effort to reassure markets amid increasing displays of credit exchanges, Mr. Kohn said recent turbulence was “partly reversed by market improvement at the end of September and during October.”
If the upward trend persisted, it would increase the possibility of additional adjustments in financial conditions for real estate and businesses, Kohn said.
The comments were taken by investors as a sign that the reserve is ready to inject fresh capital into the U.S. markets and to make subsequent interest rate cuts, to stabilize credit conditions.
Observations also helped quell Wall Street amid bad news for the U.S. economy, such as home sales that reached the lowest record in October.
The consequences of financial turbulence were accentuated by a notice from Bear Stearns, an investment bank that will cut 650 jobs, equating to 4% of its total workforce, in an overall cost reduction to offset losses of its investments in U.S. sub-prime mortgages.
Only 20 of those positions are expected to enter London, where the bank employs 1,500 people.
In New York, the Dow Jones industrial average rose by 331 points, or 2.55%, to close at 13,289.40, while the S&P 500 broad index rose 2.65%.
In London, the FTSE 100 index rose 165.5 points, or 2.7%, to close at 6,306.2, adding more than 39 billion sterlins to the value of Britain’s top companies.
Germany’s Dax index rose 2.6%, and France’s CAC40 was above 2.3%.
Previously, the recurrence of credit tension in the markets was highlighted after strong demand in a three-month auction of funds from the European Central Bank, which considered those loans to European commercial banks at 4.7%, the highest rate since April 2001.
The pressure on the Bank of England to track the performance of the federal reserve and the ECB and injecting extra funds into the stock markets in London strained credit and three-month "Libor" borrowing rates between British banks, marked the largest increase since September of 6.59%, after a steady rise since 12 November.
England takes the demands of Owais Shah and Ravi Bopara with trial
Here are two dilemmas, each as difficult as the other.
Whicho should hit No. 6 for England in Kandy this weekend, Owais Shah or Ravi Bopara?
And, none is less important in the face of the first test against Sri Lanka, who must make the decision?
The second question is relevant because David Graveney, the president of the national team who is on loan – with that particular title at least, is here to help Peter Moores, the coach, and Michael Vaughan, the captain, to decide; and also because Hugh Morris’ desk, director of England, is starting to be filled with offers to become a national selector, and if not in a part time.
The one who doesn’t get the six-at-bat will be unlucky, but life is often unfair and the sport too.
It’s not easy to pull what a man does at the top of his profession, while another never goes beyond the lower level, sometimes for lack of luck rather than capacity.
Not only in cricket the choice is difficult, because judgments that always end up being subjective are required.
Is it just enough time for Bopara to wake up?
It's fair to Shah if he does it?
The same question could be asked in another aspect if, says John Edwards or "Tubby" Banerjee, two brilliant young executives with good grades and who know the business well, they would have been promoted to the board: there's only room for one of them.
Who should say what is a good level for a cricket player, unless they give him the opportunity to sink or swim at the next chance?
The figures naturally have a great influence on decisions, but so they also have opportunities.
Within a team, a cricket player has to capture the captain’s eye at the same time of taking a “wicket” and a batter to get opportunities to change order if he wants to have an impact as a player one day.
But catching the eye of selectors is more complicated.
There were many who improved last season, for example, James Anderson, who surely plays in the first test even if Stephen Harmison declares himself for “fit and bowls” as the wind in the networks.
Anderson took 32 first-class wickets in 33 races for each to win a spot.
Chris Tremlett took only 29 on the same average, but both have had their high moments and can expect more of these.
Shah or Bopara games at Asgiriya Stadium can only be a matter of opinion.
The option can break or make a man’s career, or it can make a small difference.
Four years ago in Sri Lanka, Gareth Batty played in each test and did well in the first, with 5 wickets and 40 useful careers, but it was Paul Collingwood, struggling to establish himself at the same time who came to become a player of importance, and despite scoring a one hundred doubles against Australia in Australia, he may still be underestimated.
Graveney, Vaughan and Moores, after one more discussion in today’s session at Asgiriya Stadium, will follow their conservation instincts and favor Shah’s greatest experience over young Bopara’s exuberance.
There's arguments for both.
In Shah’s favor is his brilliant and valuable proof of debut from the early '88 in Bombay, 32 hundred first-class compared to just 6 from Bopara, who, in 22, is seven years younger, and has an excellent background against spin bowling.
But Shah did only ten runs in his two shifts in May against West Indies in the test at Lords, his Middlesex-player home, and that caused an immediate rejection of the picks when Vaughan claimed his place for the next encounter, he seemed to have taken from his “batting” something of him than in Mumbai.
By contrast, Essex’s team has the joy of living in swords.
He jumps on for nothing in the cricket field and his attitude was summarized as when he overturned his vehicle in an exhibition for England’s car sponsor, Volkswagen.
He suffered minor injuries and presumably a shock, but the first thing he did was take out his Vodafone (sponsored, of course) and call Alastair Cook, his teammate who looked in the distance, to tell him excitedly: "Did you see that?"
Bopara is the best player, he is including his slippery pass to have effects on his plays that seem to satisfy him, as he hits with confidence and skill.
Choosing the right man is one thing, choosing the best time, another.
Maybe that’s the real art of selection.
My inclination would be to give Bopara his opportunity, which does not mean that Shah does not excell, as the tea leaves suggest, it is he who plays here this weekend.
Buses bring refugees back to work - and to the fight against Al-Qaeda
The first buses with Iraqi refugees from Syria began arriving in Baghdad yesterday, expelling the exiles who were without cash, without extensions of the visa and those who waited for the city to return to normalcy.
Some had even returned to fight the militants who drove them out of their homes.
At Mansour bus station, even before the arrival of 800 people from Damascus in an official government convoy composed of a dozen buses, the terminal was full of refugees from Syria and Jordan who had returned in private transport.
"I decided to go to Syria after they killed many doctors in Baghdad," says Sabah al-Qubaisi, Sunni physician.
"I came back today because of what I saw on TV and what I read in the newspapers, about what happened in Baghdad and it's safe now."
"They say Mahdi's army stopped killing people," he declares, referring to the Shiite militia of religious militant Cleric Moqtada al-Sadr, who ordered his troops a momentary cessation.
Qassim al-Atwani, a 38-year-old Shiite and professor in the Sunni area of Amariyah, returned with his two sons because it was impossible to renew his visa in Syria because the regime does not want Iraqis working in his country.
His Shiite neighbors in Amariyah phoned him to tell him that a U.S.-allied force helped get Al-Qaeda out of his stronghold west of the capital, and urged him to return.
One of Jordan’s newcomers was the head of a Sunni and Shiite tribe that had been driven from their home in Sleikh north of Baghdad over a grenade attack, after he urged citizens to rise up against Al-Qaeda.
He had come to fight.
"Two days ago, some of my friends called me and told me that an anti-Al-Qaeda militia in Sleikh had begun and that they needed my support and that of my colleagues."
"That's why I'm back today," said Sabaah Alu Obeidi, 62.
Many said they were lured by growing calm in the capital since U.S. troops entrenched groups of “cattors concerned,” armed and working in cooperation with the U.S. military to defend their neighborhoods.
But many claimed to have returned because of the denial of visas and the lack of money in Damascus, where more than 1.3 million Iraqis settled in recent years.
Salaam al-Samarrai, a 51-year-old father of three, voiced the common suspicion among exiles that Nouri al-Maliki, the Shiite prime minister, called on the Syrian government to impose restrictions on refugee visas to force people to return.
More than two and a half million people believe they have fled the country because of the sectarian gangs of death.
"We were fine until al-Maliki visited Syria and urged the government to stop granting visas to the Iraqis, to force them to return to Iraq."
"I can't believe in a government that supports the criminals who burned down my house after I left it."
Part of the reason for the cessation of violence is that the Sunnis have been driven out of Shiite areas and vice versa, relaxing the tension.
There are fears that a sudden wave of refugees will bring up sectarian tensions again and the fighting begins.
Little Sarre, good education.
The smallest federal state is the first in vocational training and even shines by the international orientation of its higher schools.
As in the whole country, Sarlandes lack only an educational field.
"Yesterday I explained fiftieth time to a non-Sardian that we aren't French" - that was the ironic motorcycle about themselves from the smallest federal state for its fiftieth anniversary.
At the same time France and Luxembourg have a great role in the Sarre educational system that in a study of the Institute of German Economics (IW) called "Bildungsmonitor" ranked fifth.
In this way, higher schools by tradition have good relations with neighbouring countries.
Saarbrücken University was founded in 1948 by the French.
To this date the students can finish their university career here with two or three diplomas.
As a result, the percentage of foreign students is 15, 6 percent.
However, the number of students coming from Sarre is higher than average in higher schools. Many of them do not have a bacillerato.
"In Sarre the system of professional education is exploited," says Axel Plünnecke, the education sector expert at the Institute of German Economics (IW).
More than every five young people do their high school in the Professional School. Thus, it turns out that Sarre is 7 points percent above the national average.
On the contrary, the range of possibilities for a professional-academic education is very wide.
The offer of double races, in relation to the population number, is the second best in Germany.
Despite practical vocational training and increasing demand, the number of graduates in Engineering Sciences has fallen by a third in recent years.
That was the reason why the Ministry of Education of Sarre forced higher schools to do more publicity for specialties in Engineering Sciences.
“Following PISA’s first study, we carried out some reforms in general education schools,” a spokeswoman for the Ministry of Education said.
Thus the government introduced comparative tests throughout the country.
So that the children begin as soon as possible to study the last year of the project called "Kita-Jahr", it has no cost. Meanwhile, this model also mimics the other federal states.
The comparison with the next studies will show if it has increased student performance.
According to the 2003 PISA report, the number of backward pupils in school development decreased from 29 to 20 percent.
But, in general, Sarre didn’t improve much like other federal states.
In Bavaria, students know how to calculate better.
To the chagrin of the Sarlands, the PISA report does not compare the knowledge of foreign languages, although in this field they could achieve good results.
Despite the fact that the Sarlanders are not French, the language of the neighbours is a compulsory subject in Secondary Education Institutes.
Chavez changes the hour.
Soon in Venezuela there will be another time zone different from the rest of the world.
On December 9, President Hugo Chávez lets modify the schedule in the South American country delaying the clock half an hour.
Chavez is currently becoming a comedian in Spain.
As the Official Gazette of the Venezuelan State has reported, according to the new law, the time will pass at minus four GMT hours.
In this way the South American country will have a time zone that does not exist in any other country in the world.
Among other things Chávez, as an enemy of the United States, justifies his decision by saying that the United States once imposed an obligation on the world by changing a full hour.
In addition, on Wednesday (local time) Chávez announced the breakdown of relations with his neighboring country Colombia because of the action with hostage-taking.
The left-wing nationalist said he wouldn’t maintain any relationship with either the head of state or the Colombian government until conservative Álvaro Uribe remained Colombia’s president.
“My dignity doesn’t allow me to do so,” says Chavez, who is propelling a constitutional reform where the country’s citizens must decide Sunday in a referendum.
With this reform it could greatly expand its power in the country.
The reason for the conflict was Uribe’s decision to withdraw Chávez’s mandate in negotiations with the Marxist guerrilla that involved the release of the hostages.
On Sunday, Chavez said, criticizing Uribe’s decision has offended Venezuela.
There is also a lot of talk in Spain about Chavez.
A new ringtone causes sensation in the country: "Why don't you shut up?", is the phrase that half a million people have already downloaded.
This phrase was pronounced by the King of Spain who felt upset by the continuous interruptions on the part of left-wing populists at the last Ibero-American Summit.
In 2007 the decrease in the real wage threatens the Germans.
According to a forecast, this year a decrease in the real wage threatens German employees despite a strong conjuncture.
Experts present a wealth of reasons for a pessimistic forecast.
According to Hans Böckler Foundation calculations, German employees in 2007 should have a lower real wage-bearing than the previous year.
"The blame for that is the weak evolution of wages, the increase of the value added tax and the stiron of the prices of food and energy," Gustav Horn of the Institute of Macroeconomics and Research of economic cycles told the newspaper "Bild".
Also the new burdens of unemployment insurance may correspond to employees.
Finance Minister Peter Setinbrück told the newspaper "Neue Ostanbrücker Zeitung" that given the current situation, the intention to reduce the unemployment insurance burden by 3.3% after going from one year to another, is fair and reasonable.
Naturally, the work office does not have to get back into an unpleasant situation, asking for credits and subsidies to the Federal State, according to the words of a politician from the German social-democratic party.
On Tuesday it was made public that the rate of inflation with the explosion of prices in the food sector and the oil derivatives sector reached the highest level for 13 years.
Comparing prices to last year, these are increasing by 3%.
Venus: twin brother of the Earth.
Despite its atmosphere it is not suitable for life, Venus is the most Earth-liked planet, something that until now had not been proposed. The "Venus Express" European probe discovered rays in the atmosphere and swirls at the poles, which also appear in winter on Earth.
For three decades, researchers had suspected that there could be lightning on Venus.
But now the European probe has brought new evidence: it uncovered electromagnetic waves on the edge of the atmosphere. On Venus the rays stretch a height of 54 kilometers from one cloud and behind another, Cris Russell of the University of California in Los Angeles told the scientific journal "Nature".
According to Urey-Miller’s famous model of the origin of life, the rays caused life on Earth: the storms could have provoked reactions in the ocean that, for their part, would have given rise to the amino acids that are the basic components of life.
On Venus it doesn’t like this: if one day there was life there, it would have to be in one of the first states of evolution,” explains Sean Solomon of the Carnegie Institution of Washington.
Today, on the surface of Venus there can be no life: the atmospheric pressure is almost 100 times greater than on Earth and temperatures of 500°C reign on the surface, oscillating in such a way that sub-zero temperatures can be reached.
“Possibly, Venus is a “diabolical” twin of Earth, but it’s still a twin,” Russell said.
The main goal of the "Venus Express" mission is to answer the question of why Venus, so similar to the Earth for its mass and size, has had such a different evolution.
Since the 1990s there were no special missions, just for investigation of this planet, the launch of "Venus-Express" in November 2005 was a very important step for Venus investigation.
The latest results of the investigations that will be presented in many "Nature" articles, according to the researchers, underscore not only the uniqueness of Venus in the Solar System.
"This also demonstrates that neighbouring planets have more characteristics common to Earth than we had assumed," ESA reports.
Despite well-known tempestuous winds at the North Pole, the researchers discovered in the southern polar region also a vortex formed by the fastest spinning clouds.
This phenomenon is reminiscent of wind flows in the high terrestrial atmospheric layers of the poles, according to a team of Giuseppe Piccioni researchers from the Italian Institute of Special Astrophysics and Spatial Physics.
The second planet of the Solar System has the mass almost equal to Earth and with its equatorial diameter of 12,000 kilometers it is almost as large.
This planet also called alba star or evening star orbit around the Sun almost 225 days.
But its period of rotation is slower: a day on Venus equals 243 days on Earth.
Although Venus with its surface temperatures of 460 degrees and its atmospheric pressure of 92 bars does not closely resemble Earth, scientists depart from the basis that both planets had formed under the same conditions.
On the two planets there had agglomerated water that on Earth stayed in the oceans, but on Venus it dissociated into the universe.
While on Earth carbon dioxide had become carbon stones, on Venus it forms most of the atmosphere.
Koehler defended Merkel’s rally with Dalai Lama.
Koehler, the Federal President, insists on an end to the fight between parties over Dalai Lama's receival at the Federal Chancellery.
He indirectly criticized the German Social-Democratic Party for its attacks on the Federal Chancellor after the rally in September.
Horst Koehler, the Federal President, cares about Germany’s reputation and therefore demanded that the grand coalition end that fight over Dalai Lama’s reception.
"In this case we must not present ourselves as a divided nation," Koehler told "Handelsblatt."
At the same time Koehler showed his understanding regarding the reception of Dalai Lama by Angela Merkel (Christian Democratic Union Party) in September.
"Although I have great respect for the difference between cultures, human rights are absolutely valid for me," Koehler said.
Everyone knows that Germany has to act with great balance in foreign policy, emphasizes the Federal President, defending Merkel for the reproach she received in carrying out a new policy with China.
"Federal Chancellor did not take the distance from the traditional one-China policy course.
China also knows that," Koehler said.
Frank-Walter Steinmeier, the Minister of Foreign Affairs (Social-Democratic Party), had indirectly shown his disgust at the reception of Dalai Lama and because of this he was attacked by the members of the Union.
Among other things, Steinmeier criticized the Union for its inoperative policy on the outside world.
Dalai Lama’s reception in the Federal Chancellery sparked major misunderstandings in Sino-German relations.
Thus, the Chinese government not only withdrew the invitation to Peer Steinbrueck (Social-Democratic Party), the Minister of Finance of Germany, but also, according to information sources of the magazine "Spiegel", froze the so-called strategic dialogue of the Foreign Ministries of both countries.
Wen Jiabao, the Chinese head of government, demanded Federal Chancellor Angela Merkel to recognize this reception by Dalai Lama as a mistake.
Affirming China’s position, Wen Jiabao said China wouldn’t agree that European politicians would receive the Tibetan religious leader in their official position.
Dalai Lama’s actions and words showed that he is trying to divide Tibet and China’s other Tibetan region, which now also includes other provinces.
Schaeuble warns that there will be disturbances such as those in France.
Battles with the police in the streets, gunfire, burning cars: Wolfgang Schaeuble warns that riots will occur as was the case in France.
The Federal Minister demands that every effort be made so that social dissatisfaction in Germany does not take to the streets.
Barricades were made, the Vandals fired the police with rifles: after serious unrestort by young people in France, the Federal Minister of Internal Affairs, Wolfgang Schaeuble (Christian Democratic Union Party) fears similar trends in Germany.
"We also have to see that the social problems of our country do not take the violent form."
"We have to take that seriously," he said in "Neue Presse," published in Hannover.
Not all young people unfold in the constant change of social reality.
The Federal State, federal states and municipalities are already working with an emphasis to solve these problems, Schaeuble said.
This refers not only to the integration of immigrants.
"From the Integration Summit to the Conference of Islam: all this is already bearing its first fruit."
It's still early to take for granted the end of the alarm.
At the same time Schaeuble indicated that the German suburbs do not have the same building and sociological problems as in France.
The young people staged serious unrest on the streets with police north of Paris last few nights.
In Tolouse cars and public buildings were also burned.
Kenan Kolat, the president of the Turkish Community in Germany demanded greater efforts by the Federal Government for integration.
Kolat, in view of the unrest in France, warns of an imitating effect.
It is true that the situation in Germany is not as explosive as in France.
In spite of everything, performances are necessary.
Kolat indicated that the rate of unemployment among young people of foreign origin under the age of 25 is above average.
It is necessary to ensure that there are more vocational training positions.
Kolat stresses: “We have to give a perspective to young people.”
The president of the Turkish Community added that in Germany more than in France "there is great social control in families", greater than the French country.
In addition, most young people of foreign origin do not want to destroy what their parents had built here.
Kolat thinks: "But in spite of everything, we have to be careful."
It is necessary "to provide an opportunity for young people to receive a job or vocational training faster".
Meanwhile the night in the Parisian suburb of Villiers-le-Bel passed peacefully.
Hundreds of polices participated in the action, a helicopter with headlights flew over the territory.
The responsible authorities reported three arrests as well as trash and burned cars.
Since the previous day a team of almost 1000 police officers is taking care of security.
After seeing a suburb, Michelle Alliot-Marie, the Minister of Home Affairs, announced that the police will take rigorous action until it is necessary.
On Monday night around 80 police officers were injured by riots in Villiers-le-Bel.
Riots continued the next night at Tolouse.
The disturbance began with a traffic accident on Sunday, where two youths were killed.
According to information provided by the prosecution, the death of the youth was not because of the police.
Angela Wolke's kingdom.
The performance very far from the reality of the Federal Chancellor, demonstrates that the coalition in terms of content reached its last point and that we must not expect anything more from it.
But in which country does the Chancellor really live?
After her hopeless performance, indifferent and without much content in the general debate of the Lower House it must be said: she does not live in the country that would have to be governed by her.
Regarding the reproach made to him for having repeatedly devoted himself to actions in foreign policy, the Chancellor rejected it with a presumptuous phrase.
He said that that would be a provincial mode and so showed he realized that he does not want to understand what interests critics.
In the Lower House it was very clearly stated to everyone that this reproach was adequate.
You couldn’t feel a minimum interest, or any kind of feeling for the country’s problems.
But instead, Ángela Wolke presented herself in this way: a very high policy that flees in foreign policy and stays there without achieving anything in particular.
An example: the prevention of climate change: she greatly praised herself for the decisions made in Heiligendamm, but she does not say anything about how these climate goals should be realized here.
In general, this way of praising herself – for two years the head of the Christian Democratic Union Party has not been able to afford so much modesty.
Well, he insists on expressions so that an impulse will reach people.
And in this way he damages his public image by the pragmatic conception with which he solves the problems without leaving through the branches.
But in Ángela Wolke’s realm the inner problems are appearances of little value.
Children’s poverty and concerns about low-wage workers?
The only thing he devotes is talking about that topic using subordinate sentences.
It’s amazing how many times you’re mistaken to talk when it comes to the supplement for kids.
And in this case, Merkel needs more than three attempts to be able to formulate a more or less good phrase.
It begins to stumble "as if it had been in an unknown territory", when it came to more specific information.
The attacks section.
The two favorites, Rudy Giuliani and Mitt Romney, made a standoff in a heated debate of Republican YouTube presidential candidates and must now fear only one candidate from abroad.
From the outset, tensions between the candidates have been noticeable.
Mitt Romney reproached his adversary Rudy Giuliani who would have made New York "a place of refuge for illegal immigrants."
He, for his part, responded with serious reproaches: "I would like to say that his own house was a "port" for illegals." Giuliani replies to the former governor of Massachusetts saying that he was the one who employed undocumented illegal immigrants.
Candidates tend to attacks: as the group of Republicans so far does not have a clear favorite, the eight presidential candidates take advantage of the debate on CNN, especially to discredit their adversaries.
The small 30-second videos, shot by internet users who were sent to Youtube, also don’t have much content.
"With this mode of isolation we let Hitler come to power," John McCain's reproach for his adversary Ron Paul, the only one in the meeting who insisted on immediately withdrewing troops from Iraq.
Romney also receives a lot of criticism from war veterans in Vietnam, when he refuses to recognize "Waterboarding", a method of interrogation, by letting a person stifle they simulate his death.
Reactions from the public in the room in St. Petersburg in Florida show that in the meantime the most important issues of the campaign are others, not just the war in Iraq or the debates about torture.
The public always applauded very loudly when they praised conservative values and denied raising taxes.
So you can better see where are the weaknesses of both favorites.
"At that time I was wrong," explains Romney compunguado and apologizes to people and the television public for having been an advocate for abortion in 1994.
Giuliani, on the contrary, didn’t know what to say when asked what he thought about the literal interpretation of the Bible.
Musharraf rules without uniform for the first time.
A day after his resignation as head of the army, the Pakistani president was sworn in as president.
That’s the end of eight years of military rule in Pakistan.
Pervez Musharraf, president of Pakistan, was sworn in for a second 5-year term on Thursday.
Pakistani television broadcast the ceremony live at the Presidential Palace in Islamabad.
Musharraf vowed to respect the constitution and act for the well-being of the nation.
On Wednesday he transferred his term as head of the army to his confidant Ashfaq Kiyani and thus accepted international pressure, especially by the United States.
Eight years after he seized power with a bloodless military coup, the former general first rules the state as civilian chief.
With his resignation from the position of head of the Armed Forces, Musharraf fulfilled the main status of the opposition and the west.
The other demand is the end of the state of emergency, which was proclaimed by Musharraf on November 3.
According to Malik Muhammad Qayyum, the state’s attorney general, after the Musharraf oath he will soon annul the state of emergency and fulfill the second condition of the west.
Since being proclaimed a state of emergency in the Supreme Court there have been many lawyers in support of Musharraf. Last week the country's Supreme Court rejected all the appeals before the controversial new elections of the head of the government on October 6.
Meanwhile, in an attack on a convoy of the military in the Northern Watiristan region, five soldiers were killed and four others wounded.
According to the army’s spokesman, a bomb exploded in one of the streets on the border with Afghanistan.
Krautrock's founders are back.
The most famous German band of the 70's continues its activity: three members of "Harmonia" participated in a concert in Berlin.
As a restraint a copy of "Live 1974" was written in the band's best time.
Already at that time they were very famous in foreign countries.
The attendance of these three lords at an official meal caused the sensation.
Each of them has in front of itself its box, its cables and above all its buttons.
In the middle is Sitting Dieter Moebius who is 63 years old and is dressed in a white shirt.
On his left is 57-year-old Michael Rother, who has his laptop and guitar.
On the right, looking from the auditorium, is Hans-Joachim Rodelius, 73, who is very busy with the control panel.
Everyone contributes their sounds, among them there is even rhythm.
Sometimes, without any intention, these sounds become a piece of music.
Looks like this doesn't bother the gentlemen.
Something very exciting for Krautrock enthusiasts was the fact that Harmonia band made music again.
What needs to be rebelled here is this: the questions accurately have shown that the band’s name rumbled in people’s minds, but it wasn’t something so special that after 32 years the audience missed it so much.
But why the Germans cannot also applaud their living legend of the 1970s?
Particularly because the wrong Krautrock was always more valued in England and the United States than in its own country.
When the entertainment industry awarded an "Echo" music award to members of Colonia's Can 2003 band for their work of life, in the room you could see the surprised faces of German pop artists.
And when Red Hot Chili Peppers thanked La Düsseldorf und Neu!, everyone opened their mouths in awe.
Later Michael Rother spent time on stage during Red Hot Chili Peppers concerts as if he had been an old friend (of the band).
Ignorance in your country and recognition abroad are one thing.
It is a problem to remember this band, because the trio only existed two years and in those times I did not achieve a great effect on the public, however, he obtained it later thanks to the others.
Moebius and Roedelius had made music with artist Conrad Schnitzler and had a name, Cluster, who promised a lot of fame.
At first they played in the basement of Berliner Schaubuehne.
Then, for economic reasons, they worked with a music company in Duesseldorf that was patronized by a church.
For a while, Rother had a job at Kraftwerk and then worked as a guitarist in the band Neu! He managed to improve the fame of this German band who in those times was too radical.
In 1973 Rother followed Cluster's musicians in exile to Forst in Weserbergland.
There, in Alten Weserdorf, which is a 16th-century ruin, they lived as refugees from the city.
The way of life was less ideological than in Common I, where Roedelius could complain about his position.
That was rather an artist’s life full of surprises.
According to them, one day an Ottenstine Harmonia singing association flag appeared in the trash, which turned out to be a good name for art.
There were also disadvantages in the use of the name Harmonia.
The first album was called "Musik von Harmonia" and the second "De Lux".
Even though the music was quiet and simple, the band struggled financially.
Life as an artist wasn’t just to know itself.
The CD "Live 1974" was recorded during a concert in Griessem. Someone remembers that almost 50 people participated in this recording.
Then Rother expressed his desire to propel Harmony and from time to time rehearsal.
For Roedelius and Moebius this demand is against customs.
In 1975 the band announced their separation.
Rother ended up in esoteric issues and secured his existence with albums like "Flammende Herzen".
Cluster revived the 1980s and since that time has had more success than Harmonia had.
The live recording of the album in Griessmer, that must be thanked by Groenemeyer.
His music company Grienland set out to cultivate this German’s own contribution to preserve it as living music.
Like Roedelius's work, also the band Neu! was again discovered.
Harmonia’s presentation as a band takes place at the House of World Cultures in Berlin.
The band was subsidised with public measures from the Wordtronics festival.
There is Julian Cope, the Englishman, the old punk and the author of the manual "Krautrock Sampler".
Dr. Motte, Loveparade’s inventor, is also present there.
In this way, all Krautrock’s influences to the following pop music are present.
Harmonia takes the position of a super-band, a stall that was empty.
Brian Eno's phrase accompanied by special music fills the room: "This music is the only true music, the future belongs to it."
There are photos where Harmonia appears with producers on a farm.
The history of the development of the legend is shown in the projections of black and white photos, where instruments with cables are seen.
Deep down there are three veterans who started improvising again.
They do nothing more than attach the microphones to the organ and the battery to be able to produce extraordinary shades with the help of the effects devices they had built with their own hands.
Currently electronic music is how it had to be.
Three people who delve into technique.
But that doesn't change anything.
Under the direction of Barbara Morgenstern, in a musical atmosphere is coughed, whispered and in a good moment of emphasis "Aaaaaaaaah!".
Krautrock has always been an English term.
But it was never about rock.
The origin of the genre in the blues was not sought, but in the schools of music (Can), in technologies (Kraftwerk) or fleeing from artistic and social conventions (Cluster).
Whoever here shouts "68!" is also right, but now Harmonia remains calm.
On the stage he reflects very well on what is already evident.
Where does the music start and where does it end?
That can break one’s nerves or give away some quick moments of luck.
Richard Gere only lacks more cynicism.
The two films "The Hunting Party" and "Moerderischer Frieden" are again dedicated to war in the Balkans.
But they both didn’t have much conviction.
Either the concepts look a lot about actions and shootings or the two movies are too exciting.
Richard Gere with his "The Hunting Party" also can't help in this case either.
At first there was good news: Karadzic and Mladic, the war criminals, continue to hide in the Balkan mountain, but precisely there, in the dark interior of the country during the Bosnian war, actor Richard Gere was again discovered.
We had finally lost sight of the most handsome man of the 1980s, because no one wanted to see him after his pleasing and unimportant roles in women’s movies and after many press conferences about Buddhism.
Now, all of a sudden, Richard Gere is the most important argument to see perhaps the movie “The Hunting Party—when a hunter becomes what he’s after” (Why today do movie titles have to be so long and crazy?).
The film has a wonderful presentation for sight: deep down, his role as a reporter in the war is the presentation of what Richard Gere could do, if fate and the film industry had not been so merciful to him. He was a man whose best times of life have already passed, that as a monument of the ruin of his decadence preserved until now most of his charm.
"The Hunting Party", despite this triumph, along with "Moerderischer Frieden" which is German production and will also be presented this week, turn out to be just an unsuccessfull series of films about the war in the Balkans.
You can narrate the film "Moerderischer Frieden" in a few words: these are two German soldiers in Kosovo (Adrian Topol, Max Riemelt) who want to save a Serb from the snipers of Kosovo, one of them acts very quickly and that also leads him to fall in love with her.
The sense of the film is lost by actions and shootings, although we celebrate that the Serbs are not presented once again as the only culprits.
The film is like the German series "Alarm Fuer Cobra 11" without highway, but in a situation of war.
In the example of "The Hunting Party" you can investigate, because in reality there was never a good film about war in the Balkans.
At another point, with another director and another screenwriter (Richard Shepard in two counts), the story about U.S. reporters in the war leaving to detect a Serbian war criminal named "fox," could probably be a new "Apocalypse Now."
Thus, the stories are surprisingly similar: the small teams go twice to the mountain to carry out something that the army can’t do and each time it is a trip in madness.
One reason "The Hunting Party"'s failure is when we're initially introduced to Simon Hunt, who they don't let him be just a cynic with heart.
No, the script also envisages a love affair with a Muslim Bosnia that they are going to kill later.
Then "The Hunting Party" is very far from reality, especially when in the last five minutes the reporters with an impressive rapidity achieve what they could not achieve for five years the NATO, the CIA and all the hunters of heads of the world, which was to capture the war criminal and leave the Bosnian Muslims to take revenge fairly.
It’s so bad, it makes you want to appeal to the international court in The Hague based on crimes against kinematic logic.
However, the purpose of the film is, in reality, a deeply human one.
The film wants to condemn the fact that Karadžić e Mladić are still free, and stresses that the Americans and the French are not really concerned with persecuting them, because they have political arrangements between them.
Sometimes the good and the bad are so similar, so that one comes to mind a very famous poem by Brecht.
In this poem it is a mask of an Asian demon: "In the great wrinkles of his forehead I see, sharing the feelings with him, how hard it is to be bad."
In movies like "The Hunting Party" there are also these demons but they show that it's hard to be good.
The problem is not only the genre of war movies in the Balkans.
He recently published in a newspaper that all films about Iraq produced in Hollywood are fiasco.
It can also be added that there has never been a good movie about South Africa.
There's almost always a rule: when a star gets into a region of crisis or through the minefields, then something like "Rangoon" with Patricia Arguette, "On the other side of all borders" with Angelina Jolie or "Diamontes sangrentes" with Leonardo DiCaprio.
Possibly all of these conflicts occurred in a short space of time.
They have to spend at least decades until Hollywood rolls a good holocaust movie with the film "Schindler's List" as an example.
Some films already need more time. Classics such as the works about the war in Vietnam "The Deer Hunter", "Apocalypse Now" or "Full Metal Jackett" in their times were not unanimously celebrated as the masterpieces.
Today we may know too much about global conflicts, and that’s why in our times each massacre (Massenmord) is presented directly on two televisions at home.
And that’s because the script authors don’t have the slightest qualms (it’s necessary for power) to tell good stories.
"To be or not to be" or "The Great Dictator" turned out in the end so good, because Lubitsch and Chaplin still didn't know how bad the Nazi regime really was.
Maybe you shouldn’t be a good person to shoot a movie.
Perhaps you have to become like the novelist Lawrence Norfolk, with his sarcastic and distant gaze of an artist.
When he was asked about the parallels between his historical novel "Lemprière's Dictionary" and the war in the Balkans, he replied: "That's only because some things never change."
Big cities will always be in danger, men will always be cruelly killed and war will always reign in Yugoslavia.
Von Beust receives in response the negative criticism about the Olympic Games.
The mayor of Hamburg received much criticism from politics and economics for not requesting the Summer Olympics.
Many want to keep fighting for competitions in the water sports section.
But Beust remembers that you have to be realistic when estimating the possibilities.
Hans-Joerg Schmidt-Trenz, managing director of the Chamber of Commerce of Hamburg, said on Wednesday this resignation as "not good for the economic location".
In the last time no other project in the big city fostered as much motivation as the idea of the organization of the Olympic Games.
"Without this goal you couldn't maintain the political, sporting momentum at the same level."
Schmidt-Trenz demanded the senate not to forget this project, but to find a new long-term application "that refers to another time".
On Wednesday, Mayor Ole von Beust maintained his statements that Hamburg’s ambitions in organizing the Summer Olympics would not take place until at least 2028.
"Hamburg does not decide on the application for the Olympic Games, but is decided by the German Federation of Olympic Sports," von Beust says at the WELT ONLINE.
He referred to the decision of the Sportbund Federation expected in early December on Munich’s request for the 2028 Winter Olympics.
"This preference means to us that in this period of time, which until now we thought we had until 2028, from a realistic point of view we will not have any opportunity."
Despite everything, this dream didn’t end forever, von Beust said.
The Hamburg Sports Federation (HSB) regrets von Beut’s statements.
This gives the impression that in the next 25 years Hamburg does not want to apply for the Olympic Games.
"It's too soon to let go of the opportunity, if right now the plans are ruined," G�ünter Ploß, the director of HSB, said Wednesday.
A waiver of the request is contradictory to the statement of the senate chief who made at a reception on the occasion of the general meeting of the German Public Federation of Higher School Sports (adh).
There von Beust designated Hamburg's application for Universities in 2015 not as "a substitution" of the Olympic Games in Hamburg.
Only last week the Higher Schools Sports Federation named Hamburg as an application candidate.
The Fraction of the German Social-Democratic Party (SPD-Bürgerschaftsfraktion) reproached the senate who did not take advantage of such valuable time for the construction of houses in Hamburg.
"For many years the senate delayed with the development of the small neighborhood of Grasbrook, because this was in plans for the Olympic Games or the construction of houses," said Carola Veit and Jan Quast, deputies of the German Social-Democratic Party (SPD).
While the SPD always demanded an integrated planning of Olympia for little Grasbrook and then the use of houses built, the CDU underscored the importance of entertainment.
At that time, Hamburg’s request for the 2012 Summer Olympics provoked great enthusiasm.
But Hamburg failed because of Leipzig.
They're not wooden boxes.
Who relates the terms Sweden and wooden box, thinks of Ikea, but doesn’t think of Andy Warhol.
Or yes?
Currently the wooden boxes produced in Sweden are like the shadow that weighs on Warhol's list of works.
Most of the ninety-four Brillion-Box "type" on this list are fake.
After a few months ago the Swedish newspaper "Expressen" put the doubt about the authenticity of Brillo-Boxes of the year 1968 of Andy Warhol, the Moderna Museet museum in Stockholm carried out internal investigations and reached disappointing conclusions: the six "Brillo-Boxes", which the former director of the museum Pontus Hultèn gave to the museum Moderna Museet, are also false, and almost one hundred of the others of the "Brillo-Boxes"
Prehistory is too complicated with a confusing relationship between artistic and sponsored productions.
In 1964 Warhol created in New York his first "Gloss-Boxes", he painted the wooden boxes of red, blue and white, with the same sample that they had "Gloss" soap powder cartons.
According to Warhol, in the exhibition of his 1968 works in the Moderna Museet museum were exhibited hundreds of Brillion-carton boxes. These were not made by Warhol (because of the expenses), but they were brought directly from the "Brillo" cardboard factory.
They were not works of art, but of specimens (which were only objects) exhibited.
In the same year, 1968, Warhol made it possible to produce in Sweden some brilliance-wooden boxes, but they were not exhibited at the Modern Museet museum.
Witnesses of those times now speak that almost fifteen boxes, which at that time entered the history of art and the auction as originals of the "Brillo-Boxes" of "Stock Type" authorized by Andy Warhol.
In auctions some of them were bought for more than 100 000 dollars.
The problem is that after the famous director of the Pontus Hultén museum made false statements that in 1968 in Sweden were produced and exposed almost a hundred of the wooden boxes that Warhol left to him.
But in reality, 105 boxes were produced in the same year 1990 in Malmö for Hultén and for the exhibition in St. Petersburg.
Warhol had already died for 3 years.
Lars Byström, the head of curators of the Moderna Museet museum, researched six Brillo-Boxes owned by the museum and also three of the Swedish private property that were manufactured in 1968 in Stockholm. They were first printed and emerylated their tight silvers and then painted them in oil with the brush.
On the contrary, the structure of the copies of 1990 shows that the painting was applied with a roller without directly printing the tight silvers. In addition, it is not about oil painting, but about acrylic painting.
“These boxes have not been authorized by the artist and must be removed from Andy Warhol’s official Brillo-Boxes list,” says Lars Nittve, the director of the Moderna Museet museum and adds that if The “Andy Warhol Art” Authentication Commission in New York won’t come to any conclusions, the Moderna Museet museum will classify these six boxes as exhibition copies/objects.”
Already in July the Authentication Commission formally announced that it accepts these "serious accusations" in which they are boxes of soap powder.
But from that moment there everything remained in absolute silence.
Rebellious soldiers surrender in Manila.
In the Philippines have surrendered rebel soldiers who had barricaded themselves in a hotel in Manila and had demanded the fall of President Gloria Macapagal Arroyo.
Leader Antonio Trillanes, who reproaches the president for corruption and electoral fraud, told a reporter she couldn’t answer for shedding blood.
Soldiers loyal to the government had assaulted the hotel using tear gas.
Almost two hundred soldiers have been barricaded in the building for a few hours.
Almost half of them, including Trillanes, had trial on Thursday for having tried a coup d’état in 2003 that ended in failure.
He took advantage of the negotiations, so he could suddenly deposit with about 30 supporters.
Evidently the military policemen who were supposed to monitor him had passed to their ranks and followed his mutiny.
They entered the hotel "Peninsula" and demanded the fall of President Gloria Macapagal Arroyo.
Government troops blocked the neighborhood around Hotel Noble.
The president called for an urgent Security Cabinet session.
Norberto Gonzales, the National Security Advisor, condemned the soldiers’ behavior.
"People are fed up," Trillanes said.
“We called people to move into our ranks.”
General Danilo Lim, who previously led the elite Scout Reangers troops, said that the other military troops soon wanted to follow the march.
The great critics of government called Thursday for a large demonstration against the president.
For months, his government has been reproached for corruption.
In 2003 the financial district in Manila was a site that attracted full attention for the mutiny that was attempted against Arroyo.
The uprising failed after about 24 hours because the army wanted to participate.
Since 1986 in the Philippines there have been at least seven coup attempts against the democratically elected government.
United Internet will be Versatel’s largest shareholder.
United Internet secured more than 20 percent of the shares of telecommunications company Versatel.
On Wednesday the company reported that the average price for a share is 14, 46 euros.
You have only 20.05 percent of the share capital.
There was a lot of speculation about the company’s absorption.
There are also options to further increase the financial part in Versatel.
United Internet reported that with this step it wants to accelerate the consolidation of the German DSL (Digital Subscriber Line) market.
On Wednesday they already made assumptions about entry into business, after Bank Brendenberg increased its stake to Duesseldorf network operator by 5 percent to 15.1 percent.
In the financial market that means that the financial institution maintains the equity for a third-party partner.
United Internet already in the previous day made the telecommunications company Drillisch participate with almost 10 percent.
Drillisch in terms of capital increase placed 17.4 million of the new shares that have a value of 106.4 million euros.
United Internet has purchased 5.15 million shares for 31.4 million euros.
In addition, the companies began negotiations on DSL-product marketing cooperation.
"We strengthen our collaboration," the United Internet spokesman said.
The two companies founded an MSP board company to acquire competitor Freenet.
On Thursday United Internet reported that at the beginning, the two companies want to be happy with the participation of ten percent to Freenet and do not make use of their right, that is, they involve 18.49 by hundreds of Freenet.
That meanted that both companies maintained all the options, especially the participation to the Freenet corporation.
Negotiations between United Internet and Freenet over a strategic partnership ended in mid-November.
This "game" with Freenet that will last for many months and is not very understandable is part of Telecom's German market consolidation.
At the end of the third quarter Freenet had 2.9 million customers with a mobile phone contract and 1.27 million with a DSL contract or an entire contract.
Until now, the United Internet and Drillisch held directly 6.01 percent of Freenet’s stake.
Then they had agreed that Drillisch will achieve the other four percent.
The right to buy 18.49 percent of the Vatas investment company was already agreed in August.
